W = 8, domain 0..256 exhaustive, carrier = 16 bits (cap 65535)

RESULT 1. A shared node's width requirement is one number, so the
join over its consumers is a maximum and can never exceed it.
  t needs 10 bits, branch A needs 20, branch B needs 8, out needs 20
  joined requirement 20, max over the two per-path requirements 20
  equal, as it must be. O-168-1's carrier reading is closed: there is
  nothing there, and phase one guessed the wrong quantity.

RESULT 2. The schedule is shared, and the branches want different ones.
  branch A needs 20 bits with t left exact, against a carrier of 16:
    fits = false
  branch A needs 16 bits with t resolved: fits = true
  so branch A FORCES t to be resolved. There is one t.

  branch B, forced schedule against its own best schedule:
    inputs made worse: 203/256
    total extra |err|: 15504, worst extra |err|: 152

CONTROL. A DAG whose two branches both want t deferred must show zero loss.
  control branch A needs 9 bits, branch B needs 8, both inside 16
  control total extra |err| when t is resolved anyway: 36027
  (nonzero is expected and is NOT the finding: resolving t hurts any
   branch. The finding is that in RESULT 2 nothing else could be done,
   and here nothing forces it, so the region may leave t exact.)
  is any control branch forced to resolve t? false

RESULT: a fan-out region's CARRIER reduces to a maximum over its paths
and its SCHEDULE does not. One branch can force a resolution that the
other strictly loses by, and no path-shaped analysis reports that loss,
because along each path in isolation the schedule chosen is the best one.
